## Maximum Principle for the Optimal Harvesting Problem

### Answer-leak check

The problem statement contains only the problem (objective, dynamics, constraints, parameters). No solution, proof sketch, or answer is embedded. Proceeding to solve.

---

### 1. Well-posedness of the state equation

For any $u \in L^\infty(0,T)$ with $0 \le u \le M$, the state equation reads

$$x'(t) = \bigl(r - u(t)\bigr)x(t) - k\,x(t)^2, \qquad x(0)=x_0>0.$$

This is a Bernoulli/logistic-type ODE with a globally Lipschitz right-hand side on bounded $x$-sets. Since $u\ge 0$,

$$x'(t) \le r\,x(t) \implies x(t) \le x_0 e^{rt}, \qquad t\in[0,T],$$

so $x$ is bounded above. The coefficient $r-u(t) \ge r-M$ is bounded below, hence the logistic equation admits a unique global solution $x^u \in C([0,T])$ with $x^u(t)>0$ for all $t$ (because $x=0$ is an equilibrium and uniqueness prevents crossing it). In particular $x^u \in L^\infty(0,T)$ and the map $u \mapsto x^u$ is well defined on the admissible set

$$\mathcal U_{\rm ad} = \bigl\{ u \in L^2(0,T) : 0 \le u(t) \le M \text{ a.e.}\bigr\}.$$

The objective $J(u) = \int_0^T u(t)\,x^u(t)\,dt$ is therefore finite for every admissible $u$.

---

### 2. Pontryagin Hamiltonian

Introduce the adjoint (costate) variable $p \in AC([0,T])$ and define the Pontryagin Hamiltonian

$$
H(t,x,u,p) \;=\; u\,x \;+\; p\,\bigl(r\,x - k\,x^2 - u\,x\bigr).
$$

(The running payoff is $L(t,x,u)=ux$; there is no discounting and no terminal cost $\phi$, so $\phi\equiv 0$.)

---

### 3. Adjoint equation and transversality

The costate satisfies

$$
-p'(t) = \frac{\partial H}{\partial x}\bigl(t, x^*(t), u^*(t), p(t)\bigr), \qquad t\in(0,T),
$$

i.e.

$$
\boxed{\;p'(t) = -\,u^*(t) \;-\; p(t)\bigl(r - 2k\,x^*(t) - u^*(t)\bigr)\;}
\tag{adj}
$$

or equivalently $p'(t) = -p(t)\bigl(r-u^*(t)\bigr) + 2k\,p(t)\,x^*(t) - u^*(t)$.

Since the terminal state is **free** (no constraint on $x(T)$) and the terminal cost is $\phi\equiv 0$, the transversality condition is

$$
\boxed{\;p(T) = 0.\;}
\tag{trans}
$$

---

### 4. State equation (recall)

$$
\boxed{\;\bigl(x^*\bigr)'(t) = r\,x^*(t) - k\,x^*(t)^2 - u^*(t)\,x^*(t), \qquad x^*(0)=x_0.\;}
\tag{state}
$$

---

### 5. Pointwise maximization condition

A necessary condition for optimality is that $u^*(t)$ maximizes $H$ over $[0,M]$ pointwise a.e.:

$$
H\bigl(t, x^*(t), u^*(t), p(t)\bigr) \;\ge\; H\bigl(t, x^*(t), v, p(t)\bigr), \qquad \forall\, v\in[0,M],\ \text{a.e. } t.
$$

Regroup the Hamiltonian by isolating the $u$-dependence:

$$
H = \underbrace{p\,r\,x - p\,k\,x^2}_{\text{independent of }u} \;+\; u\,x\,(1-p).
$$

Since $x^*(t)>0$, $H$ is **affine** in $u$ with slope (the *switching function*)

$$
\Phi(t) \;:=\; \frac{\partial H}{\partial u}\bigl(t,x^*(t),u,p(t)\bigr) \;=\; x^*(t)\,\bigl(1 - p(t)\bigr).
$$

Maximizing an affine function over $[0,M]$ yields the **bang–bang / singular** structure:

$$
\boxed{\;
u^*(t) =
\begin{cases}
M, & \text{if } p(t) < 1 \quad (\Phi(t)>0),\\[4pt]
0, & \text{if } p(t) > 1 \quad (\Phi(t)<0),\\[4pt]
\text{any value in }[0,M], & \text{if } p(t) = 1 \quad (\Phi(t)=0).
\end{cases}
\;}
\tag{ctrl}
$$

---

### 6. Singular-arc analysis

On a singular arc we have $\Phi\equiv 0$ on a nontrivial interval, i.e. $p(t)\equiv 1$. Then from (adj),

$$
0 = p'(t) = -u - 1\cdot\bigl(r - 2kx - u\bigr) = -(r - 2kx),
$$

so $x^*(t) = \dfrac{r}{2k}$ (the logistic carrying-capacity half-point). Imposing $x'=0$ in (state) at this level:

$$
0 = r\cdot\frac{r}{2k} - k\cdot\frac{r^2}{4k^2} - u\cdot\frac{r}{2k}
\;\Longrightarrow\; u = \frac{r}{2}.
$$

Hence a singular arc is feasible only if $\dfrac{r}{2}\in[0,M]$, in which case

$$
\boxed{\;u_{\rm sing} = \frac{r}{2}, \qquad x_{\rm sing} = \frac{r}{2k}, \qquad p_{\rm sing}=1.\;}
\tag{sing}
$$

(If $r/2 > M$ the singular control is inadmissible and the optimum is purely bang–bang.)

---

### 7. Summary — the Maximum Principle

If $u^*\in\mathcal U_{\rm ad}$ is an optimal control with associated state $x^*=x^{u^*}$, then there exists an adjoint $p\in AC([0,T])$ such that, a.e. on $(0,T)$:

| Condition | Equation |
|---|---|
| **State** | $x' = r\,x - k\,x^2 - u^*\,x,\quad x(0)=x_0$ |
| **Adjoint** | $p' = -u^* - p\,(r - 2k\,x - u^*)$ |
| **Transversality** | $p(T)=0$ |
| **Hamiltonian maximality** | $u^*(t)\in\arg\max_{v\in[0,M]}\bigl\{v\,x + p\,(r x - k x^2 - v x)\bigr\}$ |

Equivalently, with switching function $\Phi(t)=x^*(t)(1-p(t))$:

$$
u^*(t) =
\begin{cases}
M, & \Phi(t)>0,\\
0, & \Phi(t)<0,\\
\text{singular } (u=r/2,\ x=r/(2k),\ p=1), & \Phi(t)\equiv 0 \text{ on an interval and } r/2\in[0,M].
\end{cases}
$$

This is the **Pontryagin maximum principle** for the given logistic harvesting problem: the optimal policy is bang–bang (harvest at the maximum rate $M$ when the shadow price $p<1$, and shut off harvesting when $p>1$), with a possible singular arc at the logistic half-carrying-capacity level $x=r/(2k)$ driven by the constant effort $u=r/2$ whenever that effort is admissible.

